Mint. &nbsp;Finally. &nbsp;I didn&#39;t count rounds, but it took ~1.5 hours. &nbsp;And most of that was clicking through mass damage.&nbsp;It turns out that Legacy with Fortitude, Lead From the Front, and 3 Energy Bracers is nigh invulnerable. &nbsp;(And Superhuman Durability can deal with the handful of things that Gloomy can do to deal 5+ damage. &nbsp;But it takes FOREVER.

[/quote]

I went basically the same route, and took just about as long. &nbsp;Near Mint (I screwed up one of AA&#39;s super-fiddly multiple instruments/power/card play turns and had to undo), but Flawless Victory -- all heroes at max HP. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

Legacy was tanked to the max, with not only three Energy Bracers but also three Autonomous Blades, Surge of Strength, Inspiring Presence, and the Legacy Ring. &nbsp;With all of the buffs and constructs, every Motivational Charge did at least 5+4+4+4=17 points of damage, and that&#39;s if I didn&#39;t have AA in there adding extra out-of-turn Galvanize uses. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

I can just imagine this playing out in the &quot;real world&quot;: &nbsp;

<em>Gloomy&#39;s little cultists obtain one of Blade&#39;s old MDPs, and use it Renfield-style to transport the corpse of their dead master. &nbsp;Using an unholy ritual, they raise him from the dead. &nbsp;Our three heroes break into the MDP and try to clean out the nest of cultists, but they have to chase them through the labrynthine corridors of the MDP. &nbsp;The cultists take full advantage to backstab, sneak attack, and place Gloomy&#39;s little voodoo pins all over the heroes, while the MDP crewmembers snipe and strafe from cover. &nbsp;It&#39;s a long, ugly, bloody fight. &nbsp;</em><em>After finally clearing out the last of the cultists, our beat-up and weary heroes emerge on the flight deck, where they are greeted by the horrifying sight of a colossal demon zombie Gloomweaver, oozing malice and baleful magic.&nbsp;</em>

<em>But here where they have room to move, the heroes&#39; teamwork finally comes into play: &nbsp;&nbsp;</em><em>Every time the demon monster aims one of his tremendous fists at Legacy, an equally massive yellow energy shield from Captain Cosmic deflects the blow. At the same time, yellow energy swords are whirling in an arc around the enraged monster, darting and slicing. &nbsp;All the while, the eerie long-haired stranger dressed in silver plays his otherwordly music, which, powered by the pure stellar energy flowing out of Captain Cosmic, is visible and tangible, as well as audible. &nbsp;The music literally lifts and sustains the heroes: &nbsp;It washes away the pain and weariness from the long fight in the corridors, their bruises visibly fade, open wounds stop bleeding and begin to knit themselves shut. They think more clearly, move more quickly, fight more creatively.&nbsp;</em>

<em>Legacy begins to go on the offensive. &nbsp;The music enhances even Legacy&#39;s own inherent super-speed. &nbsp;He becomes a stellar energy-powered blur, barely visible. &nbsp;At the same time, he&#39;s throwing cosmically- and musically-enhanced punches that could level buildings. &nbsp;The resurrected Gloomweaver is puissant and terrible, but even this&nbsp;</em><em>hellish revenant is simply no match for the onslaught of 200 years of enhanced human evolution, powered by cosmic energy and arcane melodies. &nbsp;Gloomweaver goes down in a hail of lightning-fast blows and incandescent streaks of yellow energy, his cries of rage and despair drowned out by music that seems to be coming from everywhere and nowhere at the same time. &nbsp;</em>
